all right first classic interview of the  new year we're gonna go back to my  interview with James Kilgore a former  inmate who actually understands the  prison system spoke to us about the  prison system this is from September of  2015 and as I reviewed this interview to  run it for you this week especially for  people who never saw it when it  originally aired I was thinking of  rather than how far we've come with  regard to reforming our prison system  since I did this interview back in 2015  I was thinking of the opposite how we  haven't really advanced much in terms of  our thinking and our sophistication and  our understanding about the problems  with America's prison system so that's  how I was thinking of the interview  maybe you'll think about it the same way  maybe not my interview with James  Kilgore from September of 2015 is this  week's classic interview James Kilgore  was the last fugitive member of the  Symbionese Liberation Army and he spent  six years in prison for the murder of a  Sacramento housewife during an April  1975 bank robbery the Symbionese  Liberation Army is the 1970s era group  notorious for bank robberies and  killings and the kidnapping of Patricia  Hearst and during his time in prison  James drafted his three published novels  he teaches at the University of Illinois  at urbana-champaign and his new book is  called understanding mass incarceration  a people's guide to the key civil rights  struggle of our time James joins me now  James sometimes mass incarceration and  the sort of mass incarceration status  quo in the United States is described as  a sort of experiment can you talk about  that a little bit in what ways is our  status quo with incarceration a sort of  experiment well I think it's beyond the  experimental stage it's been going on  for over 30 years  and it's a qualitative change in the way  in which the u.s. government deals with  not only criminal justice but poverty  more broadly so we've seen that prison  and jail population expand from less  than half a million in 1980 to over 2.2  million today we've seen the budgets  that amount of money that's spent on  incarceration from grow from about six  billion to over eighty billion today so  I think we're beyond the experimental  stage I think it's become really part of  the lay of the land I think Brian  Stevenson the the author of just mercy  and excellent book on this topic has  said that mass incarceration now defines  us as a society I don't think it's an  experiment unfortunately and it's going  to take a very powerful social movement  and a very powerful set of policy  changes in order to end this established  practice no I certainly agree with that  I guess the the I used the term  experiment more in the sense that we we  didn't really have any data telling us  that some of the sort of stated  objectives of the incarceration status  quo would actually reduce crime and we  were sort of being told source old this  bill of goods that hey listen we get we  get criminals off of the street you  prevent them from committing crimes in  the future the war on drugs which was  incredibly racially tinged was presented  as a way to sort of prevent nonviolent  drug offenders and low-level drug  dealers from sort of graduating to more  serious crime and didn't it fail in the  sense that in many ways the  incarceration status quo may be creating  more crime than it prevents absolutely  the the expansion of prisons and jails  has created havoc in many communities in  the country  Eric Ghidorah has come up with the idea  of million dollar blocks where the state  is spending more than a million dollars  on locking up people from blocks and  specific in specific neighborhoods these  are neighborhoods made up largely of  poor people of color not only have  thousands of people been taken from  these communities to prisons and jails  but also the social welfare provision in  those communities has been has been cut  back so the means of economic and social  survival in those communities has been  made more difficult and to top that off  we've seen an acceleration of police  these communities and the pouring of  dollars into resources to develop SWAT  squads to arm police with m16s and  armored personnel carriers and so forth  all of this has created an environment  where we're much more likely to have  crime or much more likely to be  provoking arrests and we're much more  likely to have policing that looks to  arrest and punish people rather than to  solve community problems so I want to  talk a little bit about some of the  changes we've seen over the last 10 to  15 years and I've talked many times on  my program about how 9/11 and the sort  of desire for security that it caused  was successfully used to pass everything  from the Patriot Act to scale up NSA  surveillance etc you write in your book  about how 9/11 was also used to  intensify the war on drugs and I think  maybe people are less familiar with how  that works so tell us a little bit about  that well I think 9/11 contributed to  the kind of internationalization of the  war on drugs and blaming some of the  elements of excessive drug use in the  communities on what was happening in  Mexico and South America so that then  also connected to an anti-immigrant  policy which we see today and that is in  in since the the year 2000 we've seen in  a tremendous increase in deportations  we've also seen criminalization of  immigrants on the guys that they are  likely to be bringing in drugs but the  the population for example of Latinos in  the prison system has increased by 50  percent since the year 2000 a much  faster growth rate than any other  population group we've also set up  special prison prisons criminal alien  prisons which are set up specifically  for people who have immigration offenses  and are labeled criminals and made to do  time in many cases even for simply  crossing the border people can do up to  ten years even up to 20 years if they  prior arrests previously when people  were convicted of illegal entry they  were simply taken to the border and sent  back across now people can do years in  prison for offenses like that and even  though private prisons make up a  relatively small portion of the total  number of prisons in the United States  the private prisons are  disproportionately capitalizing on the  detention of undocumented immigrants and  this has become a huge moneymaker has it  not absolutely the private prisons david  have found immigration to be their  market niche so they hold only about 9%  of prison beds across the country but  about 40% of the immigration detention  beds and this has become their this is  really Salvage them at the turn of the  century private prisons were in very  difficult financial straits there were a  number of scandals going on  but Steve Cornell who was the head of  one of the private prison companies at  the time said that after 9/11 that  immigration was going to be the future  of private prisons and he's been proven  correct not only are they building  detention facilities but they also spend  a lot of time lobbying federal and state  governments for harsher immigration laws  in order to increase the numbers of  people that are held in detention  they are also contracting for  supervision of people who are awaiting  immigration judicial procedures so for  example the the bi incorporated which is  a subset of the geo group the second  biggest private prison provider they  have a contract to keep 7,000 people on  electronic monitors who are awaiting  decisions about asylum or deportation  applications so really immigration is  that is their baby and they're trying to  promote harsher laws and they also  participated in in the in advocating for  the laws in Arizona Alabama Mississippi  and so forth that have tightened up  immigration policy is there an  opportunity today where we find  ourselves today with regard to  incarceration to start undo  some of the elements that have  contributed to what we now see is there  anything in particular going on right  now that might serve as a sort of  entryway to starting to shake up what  has clearly been a failed system I think  there's a lot going on things that we  haven't seen previously and I'd just  like to mention two the first is at the  top we've begun to see some bipartisan  discourse against mass incarceration  mass incarceration has become a  political issue Hillary Clinton's  talking about it  Newt Gingrich's talking about it the  president has talked about it  it wasn't even mentioned in the 2012  political campaign and now across the  board you're seeing partnerships Rand  Paul joining hands with Cory Booker and  so forth to try to find ways to reduce  the jail and prison population in many  instances simply because too much money  was being spent on Corrections to the  detriment of state and local governments  but secondly and I think more  importantly we've seen huge amounts of  mass action across the country at the  community level and at the national  level and and I don't think we can  divorce all of the activities that have  taken place in response to the killings  of Michael Brown and Eric garner Tamir  rice and so forth from the whole system  of mass incarceration and a racialized  criminal justice system and connected to  that has been the resurgence of  immigrant rights activities forcing the  president to modify his stance on  deportation and to relieve five million  people from immediate threat of  deportation  the government's reactions to these  phenomenon are largely because there's a  fear that if they don't take some kind  of ameliorative action we'll end up with  uprisings across the across the country  so I think apart from those uprisings  there's hundreds of projects and  campaigns across the country against  private prisons against cash bail  against prison building against jail  building and programs for people  returning from prison a whole host of  things that are being addressed by  people who are on the move in a way  we've never  seen before in the last two decades I'll  remind you that the book is  understanding mass incarceration of  people's guide to the key civil rights  struggle of our time we've been speaking  with the book's author James Kilgore  James really a pleasure to have you on  thanks very much David
 

